   "ap": true,
   "point": "Kampung Sungai Chinchin, Gombak (OpenStreetMap via Nominatim); the harms recorded here are spread across villages in Gombak Utara, including Desa Mukmin and Kampung Sungai Salak",
   "start": 2017,
   "end": null,
   "mechanism": "project",
   "lenders": [
    {
     "name": "China Eximbank",
     "role": "Preferential buyer's credit to Malaysia Rail Link Sdn Bhd for Phase 1 of the East Coast Rail Link, signed on or around 1 April 2017. AidData's records, read on 15 November 2026, split it in two: record 85858, the part still being drawn, RM 37.7 billion, commitment date 1 April 2017; and record 73473, the cancelled part, RM 37.4 billion, commitment date 17 August 2017. Both records give a 20-year maturity, a 7-year grace period, a fixed 3.25% interest rate and a grant element of 46.99%, and name Malaysia's Ministry of Finance as guarantor. Record 49309 holds a later pledge for Phases 1 and 2. As of August 2018 the borrower had drawn RM 19.69 billion, a disbursement rate of 50.3%, of which RM 10.02 billion was an advance payment to the contractor and RM 9.67 billion a progress payment; the amount outstanding was about US$3.264 billion (RM 13.6 billion) on 31 December 2018 (AidData, records 85858 and 73473)."
    }
   ],
   "conditions": "The credit was to pay 85% of the cost of the 1 November 2016 commercial contract between Malaysia Rail Link and China Communications Construction Company, worth RMB 72.8 billion (RM 46 billion), so the money was spent with the lender's own state contractor. 60% was to be made available in renminbi and 40% in ringgit. The Government of Malaysia gave a sovereign guarantee. When Malaysia cancelled the project in August 2018, China Eximbank told the new government it could either renegotiate the contracts or pay termination costs of about RM 21.78 billion; the government renegotiated, and a supplemental agreement on 12 April 2019 cut the cost from RM 65.5 billion to RM 44 billion and set up a 50:50 joint venture with the contractor to run the line (AidData, records 85858 and 73473).",
   "companies": [
    {
     "name": "China Communications Construction Co. (CCCC)",
     "role": "Engineering, procurement, construction and commissioning contractor for the railway, working in Malaysia as China Communications Construction (ECRL) Sdn Bhd, which is one of three defendants in a 2026 High Court claim over damage to a house at Gombak (AidData; New Straits Times)",
     "hq": "Beijing, China",
     "hq_lat": 39.91,
     "hq_lng": 116.36,
     "desc": "Chinese state-owned transport infrastructure group, incorporated on 8 October 2006 and founded by China Communications Construction Group (CCCG). It designs and builds ports, roads, bridges, railways and tunnels and also does dredging, heavy machinery manufacturing and international contracting, with more than 60 subsidiaries as of 2019."
    },
    {
     "name": "Malaysia Rail Link Sdn Bhd (MRL)",
     "role": "Borrower and owner of the railway; paid the ex-gratia money at Desa Mukmin and agreed to cover rent for 21 households and repairs to homes and roads at Sungai Chinchin (AidData; Sinar Harian; Selangor Journal)",
     "hq": "Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia",
     "hq_lat": 3.139,
     "hq_lng": 101.6869,
     "desc": "A state company set up on 26 September 2016 as a wholly owned subsidiary and special purpose vehicle of Minister of Finance Incorporated, the corporate arm of Malaysia's Ministry of Finance that holds assets and signs contracts for the government. It was created to design, finance, build and maintain the East Coast Rail Link, and owns the line during construction (AidData)."
    }
   ],
   "community": "A census by Selangor's Department of the Director General of Lands and Mines on 21 November 2023 confirmed 135 structures in the railway's path at Desa Mukmin in Gombak, holding 157 heads of household. An order to clear the site was issued on 13 December 2023, with a deadline of 15 January 2024. Each household was offered RM 3,000 in moving costs, RM 1,500 a month in rent for 24 months and payment for the structure, plus a choice of 49 state flats at Bandar Tasik Puteri priced at RM 79,000 or places in two rental schemes (Sinar Harian). Residents said they were told in late November 2023 that they had 20 days to leave, and about 100 homes would go (BenarNews). By February 2024, 151 homes had been demolished and about 2,500 residents of 13 villages in Gombak said they would seek a court injunction (Free Malaysia Today, citing a news report). At Kampung Sungai Salak, named residents said flash floods started after trees and the hillside behind the village were cleared, and one said flood water came through her kitchen door. At Kampung Sungai Chinchin, piling work that began on 21 September 2023 less than 100 metres from houses was followed by cracked walls and broken floor tiles in several homes and in the Tabika Kemas Batu 7 kindergarten, whose teacher said the cracks appeared after the piling started (Malaysiakini). On 21 October 2024 the Selangor chief minister said flash floods had resulted from the construction, that 21 houses in low-lying areas would be moved to rented homes at Malaysia Rail Link's cost, and that the company had agreed to pay for damage to homes and roads caused by blasting; he also listed noise, air and dust pollution and burst pipes (Selangor Journal).",
   "displaced_note": "Sinar Harian gives 135 structures and 157 heads of household in the alignment at Desa Mukmin, under an order to vacate by 15 January 2024. Free Malaysia Today, citing another news report, says 151 homes had been demolished by February 2024. The first counts structures and household heads ordered to move, the second counts houses, so neither is recorded here as a count of people moved.",
   "suppression": "",
   "killed": 0,
   "killed_note": "",
   "ecosystem": "Residents at Kampung Sungai Salak said flash floods began after trees and the hillside behind the village were cleared for the works, and that floods now follow within an hour of rain (Malaysiakini). A private geologist engaged by one Gombak family in August 2024 reported high water saturation in the slope above their house, and a later soil study commissioned by the family concluded the chance of the slope collapsing was very high (New Straits Times).",
   "outcome": "Malaysia Rail Link handed the ex-gratia payments to the 157 Desa Mukmin households at a ceremony on 14 December 2023 (Sinar Harian). A retention pond was ordered for Sungai Chinchin in October 2024, and the contractor agreed to stop work on Sundays and during school examinations (Selangor Journal). A couple at Gombak filed a RM 1.33 million claim in the High Court on 24 April 2026 against China Communications Construction (ECRL) Sdn Bhd, Malaysia Rail Link and the Selayang Municipal Council, saying hillslope work since early 2024 made their house shake; they said three other houses were affected, a letter of demand in October 2025 was refused, and the defendants had not answered requests for the environmental assessment and site investigation reports. The paper said it had asked all three defendants for comment (New Straits Times, July 2026). Passenger services are scheduled for 2027.",
